<h1>Orcraft Test Suite</h1>
<p>A suite is the top of the hierarchy - the thing the controller loads, the thing a CI pipeline runs. It
  holds groups and nothing else, and like a group it is declared rather than coded.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestSuite,
	OrcraftTestSuiteConfig,
	OrcraftTestSuiteResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)

from my_first_group.my_first_group import MyFirstGroup
from my_second_group.my_second_group import MySecondGroup


class MySuiteConfig(OrcraftTestSuiteConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Suite")
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default="Covers the system under test end to end."
	)
	version = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="0.0.1")
	groups = OrcraftConfigField.Groups(
		default_factory=lambda: [
			MyFirstGroup(),
			MySecondGroup(),
		]
	)


class MySuiteResult(OrcraftTestSuiteResult):
	pass


class MySuite(OrcraftTestSuite):
	def __init__(self) -> None:
		super().__init__(config=MySuiteConfig())
		self.result = MySuiteResult()


def main() -> None:
	MySuite().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<h2 id="groups-field">Groups field</h2>
<p><code>groups</code> uses <code>default_factory</code> for the same reason
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/groups/#tests-field"><code>tests</code></a> does: it constructs
  objects, and a factory keeps two suite instances from sharing them.</p>

<p>The suite file is also where the suite's import graph comes together - each group imported from its own
  package. Keeping one group per folder, imported by its suite, is what lets the controller present the
  suite as a tree that matches the layout on disk.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Config fields</h2>
<h3 id="config-code">Overridable in code</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, fixed for the run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Test Suite"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the suite does. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Suite version, recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>groups</code></td>
      <td><code>[]</code></td>
      <td>The groups this suite runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>parallel</code></td>
      <td><code>True</code></td>
      <td>Whether the suite's groups may run concurrently.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td><code>HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config table, or <code>OFF</code> to leave it out of the log.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Redeclare a field with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default -
    <code>name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Suite")</code>. A bare assignment such as
    <code>name = "My Suite"</code> is not an override: it shadows the field with a plain attribute, and the
    GUI, the log table and <code>required</code> validation no longer see it.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="config-gui">Overridable in code or GUI</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, and changeable again from the run configuration panel before a run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>upload_test_package</code></td>
      <td><code>"if_missing"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the test package is uploaded to the endpoint before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>update_endpoint_app</code></td>
      <td><code>"defer"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the endpoint application is updated before the run. <code>defer</code> leaves the decision to the level below.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Same rule as above: redeclare with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default,
    never a bare value. The default you set here is what the run configuration panel shows until someone
    changes it there.</p>
</div>


<h3 id="config-reserved">Reserved</h3>
<p>Written by the framework while a run is in progress.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>start_time</code></td>
      <td>When the run started. Stamped by the framework at run time - leave it alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_tab</code></td>
      <td>Log indentation. Assigned by the framework at every level - it cannot be overridden, only collided with.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under one of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and the log indentation, the run timestamps and the
    endpoint a step is dispatched to silently stop meaning what the controller expects.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="policies">Policy inheritance</h2>
<p><code>upload_test_package</code> and <code>update_endpoint_app</code> exist on suites, groups and
  tests, and the level that speaks last does not win - the level that speaks <em>first</em> does. A
  concrete value at suite level overrides every group and test beneath it, which is what makes a
  one-off run reproducible: set <code>always</code> at the suite and nothing below can quietly skip the
  upload.</p>

<p><code>defer</code> is the opposite instruction: express no opinion and let the level below decide. It
  is the default for suites and groups precisely because a suite is a collection of tests with different
  needs, and overriding them all should be a deliberate act.</p>

<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code> takes one of four values at suite level:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>defer</code></td>
      <td>Express no opinion and leave the decision to the level below. The default for a suite.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>upload_test_package</code> has no <code>defer</code>:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="results">Result fields</h2>
<p>Like a group, a suite has no body: its result is written entirely by the framework as its groups
  complete, and an empty <code>OrcraftTestSuiteResult</code> subclass reports everything below. It
  aggregates three levels of counters at once:</p>

<p class="docs-legend"><span class="mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</span> overridable by user (optional)
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</span> written by the framework, may still be set directly
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</span> reserved</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Inherited field</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Overridden by user (optional)</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Reserved</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td><code class="status-pass">PASS</code>, <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> or <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>, derived from the children.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">failure_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Why the system under test was judged incorrect. Setting it moves a passing result to <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-error">error_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Why the component could not complete.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-warning">warning_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Something worth flagging that does not change the status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>When the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>How long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally, vertically or not at all (<code>OFF</code>). Defaults to vertical.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_groups</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_groups</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_groups</code>, <code class="status-error">error_groups</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_groups</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Group counters, summed from the groups that ran.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_tests</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_tests</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_tests</code>, <code class="status-error">error_tests</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_tests</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Test counters, summed from every test in every group.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_cases</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_cases</code>, <code class="status-error">error_cases</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_cases</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Case counters, summed from every case of every test in the suite.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>The only field that is yours here is <code>log_orientation</code>, and only its default: redeclare it
    with the same <code>OrcraftResultField</code> type -
    <code>log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL)</code>
    - never as a bare value, which shadows the field so the log table no longer sees it.</p>
</div>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under any of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and status comparisons, the three levels of counters, and the controller's result columns silently break. Only
    <code>log_orientation</code> is meant to be overridden, and only to change its default.</p>
</div>

<p>Carrying case counts all the way to the top is what makes a suite summary meaningful: two suites can
  both report ten passing tests while one executed ten cases and the other four hundred.</p>

<h2 id="running">Execution</h2>
<p>The same file serves all three ways of running:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Where</th>
      <th>How</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Standalone</td>
      <td>Execute the suite file directly, via its <code>main()</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Controller GUI</td>
      <td>Add the suite file to the controller and configure the run from the panel.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CI</td>
      <td>Run the controller in CLI mode against a CI snapshot exported from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The CI snapshot is what makes the third reproducible: it carries the configuration exactly as it was
  set in the GUI, so a pipeline reruns a configured suite rather than a differently configured one. See
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/">Setup</a> for how the controller and its CI runner fit
  together.</p>
